WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.480
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.420
Today we're learning more
about Roe versus Wade,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.000
the 1973 Supreme Court case

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.720
that ruled that the
right of privacy extends

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.240
to a woman's decision to have an abortion.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.130
To learn more about Roe versus Wade,

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.350
I spoke to two experts on the case.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.840
Clarke Forsythe is senior counsel

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.800
for Americans United for Life

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.190
and the author of Abuse of Discretion:

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.490
The Inside Story of Roe Versus Wade.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.950
Melissa Murray is the Alexander
F. And May T. Morrison

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.550
Professor of law at Berkeley Law School

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.050
where she also serves
as the faculty director

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.780
for the Center on Reproductive
Rights and Justice.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.310
Mr. Forsythe, could you set
the stage for us a little bit?

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.400
What was going on at this time period?

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.140
- [Clarke] Well, there
were efforts in the 1960s

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.920
to repeal abortion laws in the states,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.920
and when abortion
activists were dissatisfied

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.170
with those efforts, they
decided to go into the courts.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.170
And around 1969, they took
some cases into the courts,

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.310
and ultimately there were 20 or more cases

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.510
challenging state laws in the courts

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.560
between 1969 and 1973.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.330
And Roe versus Wade was a case from Texas.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.300
- [Melissa] Roe was
litigated in the early 1970s.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.400
It was a period of enormous
change in the United States.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.740
We were beginning to see the beginnings

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.150
of the Women's Rights Movements,

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.210
the beginnings of the Gay Rights Movement,

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.500
and, of course, the Civil
Rights Movement of the 1960s

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.410
was moving in a lot of
different directions.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.130
At the time, the question of abortion

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.870
was very much on the minds of lots

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.490
of different state legislatures

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.780
because there had been moves to liberalize

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.450
much of the criminal law
that dealt with matters

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.250
of sex and sexuality, including abortion.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.040
At the time, four
states, New York, Alaska,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.760
Hawaii and I believe Washington
had actually taken steps

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.610
to repeal their laws
criminalizing abortion,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.710
and about 13 other
states had taken efforts

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:15.280
to liberalize their laws
criminalizing abortions,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.280
but in a number of other
states, at least 20 or more,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.350
there remained on the books laws

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.640
that absolutely criminalized abortion

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.070
except in situations where
it would be necessary

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.890
to preserve the woman's health or life

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.120
or in cases of rape,
incest or fetal anomaly.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.330
- [Clarke] Abortion rights attorneys

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.907
sought plaintiffs who could
challenge the Texas law

00:02:41.907 --> 00:02:43.190
and the Georgia law.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.610
There were two attorneys from Texas

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.610
who found Norma McCorvey who
gave the pseudonym of Jane Roe

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.100
for purposes of protecting her privacy,

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.790
and she became the nominal plaintiff.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:03.590
- [Melissa] And so Norma
McCorvey brought this case.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.720
She was a 22 year-old woman
living in Dallas County, Texas

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.500
who found herself pregnant
for the third time.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.970
She was unmarried.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.870
Her first child has been born a daughter,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.510
and she'd ultimately signed over custody

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.500
of her daughter to her mother to raise

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.550
because she was having a
bit of an itinerant life,

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.780
wasn't able to take care of her child.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.560
The second child that she
bore she gave up for adoption.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.320
And so when she found herself
pregnant for a third time,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.760
she wasn't willing to do
either of these things again

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.080
and wanted to safely and
legally terminate her pregnancy,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.140
but this was impossible
under the Texas law.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.740
Texas had since the 19th century

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.710
absolutely criminalized abortion

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.750
except in cases where it was necessary

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.770
for the health and safety of the mother.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.670
And so she then was
faced with the question

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.210
of what was she going to do?

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.140
And the only thing she
could think to do then

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.170
was to actually challenge the law.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.550
So she was put in contact
with Sarah Weddington

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.900
and Linda Coffee, two young
women who had recently graduated

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:04.733
from law school.

00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:07.100
Sarah Weddington was only 26 years old

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.630
at the time that she helped
Norma McCorvey bring this case.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.580
But they decided to sue the state of Texas

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.120
to challenge the constitutionality

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.330
of Texas's criminal abortion ban.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:21.750
- [Clarke] But as the history shows,

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.030
there was no trial, there was no evidence,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.860
there were no expert witnesses.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.920
Jane Roe never testified.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.460
As you know, she never got an abortion.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:35.450
She gave birth and placed
her child for adoption.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.180
- [Kim] Okay, so Roe was Norma McCorvey.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.310
Who was Wade?

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.540
- [Clarke] Henry Wade
was the district attorney

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.540
for Dallas, Texas where the case was filed

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.900
in federal district court.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:55.230
- [Kim] So this case, I
assume kind of winds it way

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.310
through the courts,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.910
and how did the Supreme Court rule?

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:04.140
- [Clarke] The justices declared the Texas

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.870
and Georgia laws unconstitutional

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.310
and then rewrote a national law,

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.300
a national abortion law in which they said

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.640
that the states could not
regulate or limit abortion

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.270
in the first trimester.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.870
They could regulate more
in the second trimester,

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.410
the second three months of pregnancy

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.750
to protect maternal health,

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.280
and they could regulate
in the last three months

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.060
of pregnancy, the last trimester

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.810
to protect maternal health or fetal life.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.230
The attorneys for the plaintiffs claimed

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:44.200
that abortion fell within
the right to privacy

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.700
even though privacy is not in
the text of the Constitution.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.840
They said it was derived or based

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.700
in the language of the 14th
Amendment of the Constitution

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:55.810
even though the 14th
Amendment doesn't say anything

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.150
about abortion or the unborn child.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.800
It just uses the term liberty.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.585
And ultimately the Court said
that the right to abortion

00:06:04.585 --> 00:06:06.300
was part of the right to privacy

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.970
based on the 14th Amendment.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:08.820
- [Kim] That's very interesting.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.040
So I've learned through
many of these interviews

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.950
that this right to privacy is something

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.440
that is never actually explicitly stated

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.490
throughout the Bill of Rights,

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.980
but there's a penumbra of privacy

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.700
that you see in a few ways.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.130
What was the court's reasoning

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.370
that it was abortion

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:28.660
that could fall under
this zone of privacy?

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.830
- [Clarke] If you read the Roe opinion,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.403
on page 152 of the Roe opinion,

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:37.099
Justice Blackmon starts
out by saying that,

00:06:37.099 --> 00:06:41.730
he sites a string of
cases since about 1910,

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.409
a string of Supreme Court cases

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:46.700
and says that these lead
to the right of privacy,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.120
and we think abortion or
that the right of privacy

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.740
is broad enough to encompass abortion.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:56.490
But then four pages later on page 156,

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.360
Blackmon turns around and says,

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.140
but abortion, because it
involves the taking of a life,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.590
is inherently different
from all those other cases

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.510
that make up the right of privacy.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.210
- [Melissa] So the right of
privacy doesn't actually come

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:12.580
from Roe versus Wade.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.400
It comes from a case decided
about eight years earlier

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.910
in 1965 called Griswold
versus Connecticut.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.610
In Griswold, at issue was
a Connecticut state statute

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.310
that made it a crime to use contraception

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.270
or even to counsel patients
about contraceptions.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:32.900
Planned Parenthood League of Connecticut

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.720
opened up a birth control clinic
in New Haven, Connecticut.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.650
They were promptly arrested,
and the clinic was closed,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.920
and then they were able
to bring this case.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.990
And they argued that the right to be able

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.650
to use contraception was
the right of the individual,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:49.500
the right of the doctor to advise patients

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.220
about contraception was
also an individual right.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.180
And the Court in an opinion authored

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.320
by William O. Douglas agrees with them,

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.750
and the Court articulates
for the first time

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.390
this right of privacy,

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:06.690
and this is a right that the
majority in Griswold says

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.230
has actually been percolating
in the Court's decisions

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:10.103
for a some time.

00:08:10.103 --> 00:08:13.620
- [Kim] Did any of the justices
dissent in the Roe decision,

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.430
and if so, why?

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.950
- [Clarke] Well, there were two dissents

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:22.460
by Justice White and by Justice Rehnquist,

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.820
and Justice White said that

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:30.900
the Court was engaging
in raw judicial power

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:36.140
and that the justices
did not have the right

00:08:38.132 --> 00:08:41.830
or the authority to strike
down the abortion laws

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:45.740
of the states and could not rely upon

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.440
a doctrine called substantive due process.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.523
Justice Rehnquist said
that there us clear,

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:58.520
historical evidence that many
states passed abortion limits

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:01.030
and prohibitions precisely at the time

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:05.080
of the framing of the 14th
Amendment in the 1860s

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:09.670
leading up to 1868 and that
the evidentiary history,

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:12.850
this history of state limits
and prohibitions on abortion

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.700
contradicted any proposition
that the 14th Amendment

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.360
was intended to include
a right to abortion,

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.860
and that was kind of the
heart of his dissent.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:25.080
- [Kim] So Roe was not the last word

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.030
on abortion in the United States.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:28.570
There have been several later cases

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:29.980
that were important to this, as well,

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.970
like Planned Parenthood versus Casey

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.350
or Whole Woman's Health
versus Hellerstedt.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.530
Can you talk a little
bit about how those cases

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.940
have altered the scope
of the right to abortion?

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.550
- [Melissa] As soon as
Roe is decided in 1973,

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.240
there is an effort to sort of roll it back

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.120
and hem it in a little bit.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.580
Frank Church, who's a senator from Idaho,

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.780
announces the Church Amendment,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.020
which basically says that physicians

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.820
don't have to perform abortions

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.730
if doing so would conflict
with their conscience

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.090
or conscience beliefs.

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:03.090
So again, that's one opportunity

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.220
to sort of limit the reach of this right

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.930
by limiting the number of providers

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.840
who are available to offer abortions.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.520
- [Clarke] In fact, the
Court has kind of cut back

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.160
on Roe versus Wade in
four cases over the years.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.130
Harris versus McCrae,
involving abortion funding,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:25.010
Planned Parenthood versus
Casey, and in other cases,

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.840
they've given more
deference to the states,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:30.730
allowed the states to
pass more and more limits

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.020
at least around the margins

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.757
even though they've continued holding

00:10:36.842 --> 00:10:39.460
to the basic right that Roe created

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:40.870
that there is a right to abortion

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:44.420
for virtually any reason
at any time of pregnancy.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:46.940
That's still the scope of the right,

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:48.980
but they've allowed marginal regulations

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.810
like limits on public funding,

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.940
parental notice and consent,
informed consent laws.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:58.940
But then the Court flip flopped
in 2016, in June if 2016

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.690
in Whole Woman's Health
versus Hellerstedt.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.320
- [Melissa] The case makes its way

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.373
to the Supreme Court, and in an opinion

00:11:06.373 --> 00:11:09.190
that authored by Justice Stephen Breyer,

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:10.890
and it's only an eight person court

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.730
because Justice Scalia passes
away in February of 2016,

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.790
so just eight people on the Court.

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:19.130
In this decision that's
a five to three decision,

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:21.460
Justice Stephen Breyer notes

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.230
that the provisions that were challenged

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:28.010
do not offer the medical
benefits that they claim to offer

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.280
sufficient to justify
the burdens on access

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.460
that each of those provisions imposes.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.260
- [Kim] What do you see as
the future of Roe versus Wade?

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.487
- [Clarke] Well, the Court has failed

00:11:38.487 --> 00:11:39.500
as a national abortion control board.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:42.530
It cannot monitor abortion.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.330
It can't intervene.

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:47.360
It can't regulate or legislate itself.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.130
It can't act as public
health administrators.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.960
It can't investigate.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.530
And I believe it's absolutely certain

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:57.920
that the Court, sooner or later,

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:01.410
will have to overturn the
Roe versus Wade decision

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.370
because of this failure and
return the issue to the states.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:06.630
- [Melissa] When we are
talking about repealing

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:10.330
or reforming these laws
in the 1960s and 1970s,

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:12.640
it's also around a social movement

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.590
where one of the critical questions

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:17.600
is what will be the role
of women going forth

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.490
in a modern society?

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:22.610
When the questions of contraception
come before the Court,

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.560
one of the questions is
whether women will be allowed

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.170
to choose when and how to have children,

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.890
whether they can space
the timing of births

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.530
to accommodate careers.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.030
It's the same issue that
comes up in abortion.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.610
Like this is allowing women freedom

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.470
to be able to go into the workforce,

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.750
to determine when and how
they will become mothers,

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:44.670
and so it's not surprising
that the same questions

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.420
that arose in the 19th century
about the place of women,

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:49.230
about what happens in a society

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.890
that's undergoing change,
whether it's immigration

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:54.520
or changes in the
demography of the country,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.620
are also coming up in the 1960s and 1970s

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.640
at a time of incredible social change,

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:03.020
and I think abortion and
these rights involving

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:05.250
a woman's role really do come to the fore

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.000
and are incredibly controversial.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.940
- [Kim] So we've learned that the decision

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.820
to legalize abortion in Roe versus Wade

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:13.870
was based on the right of privacy,

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.160
which the Court has inferred
from the due process clause

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.520
of the 14th Amendment.

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.310
Since the Roe decision,
a number of other cases

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:24.200
have set limits on abortion
and abortion clinics.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.710
Clarke Forsythe argues
that the Supreme Court

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:28.710
has failed in regulating abortion

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.090
and that the issue should
be returned to the states.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:33.030
Melissa Murray, by contrast,

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.990
suggests that the
decision in Roe is crucial

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:38.440
to giving women the freedom
to join the workforce

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.660
and make decisions about
when to have children.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.360
To learn more about this case,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.400
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.520
Interactive Constitution
and Khan Academy's resources

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.453
on US government and politics.

